"If my app is in US and user is in India, eventually the request still needs to reach the US. So how does Front Door reduce latency?"

The answer is:

Front Door does not magically move your application closer to the user.
It reduces latency in other ways.

Let's break it down.

Case 1: Application Gateway Only

Suppose:

User (India)
      |
      |
Internet
      |
      |
Application Gateway (US)
      |
      |
App Service (US)

When user sends a request:

India ---> Entire Internet ---> US

The traffic travels through multiple ISP networks.

Problems:

Unpredictable routing
Network congestion
Packet loss
Multiple hops

Latency may be:

250ms - 350ms
Case 2: Azure Front Door
India User
      |
      |
Nearest Microsoft Edge (India)
      |
      |
Microsoft Global Backbone
      |
      |
US Backend

Now:

India ---> Chennai/Mumbai Edge

This part is short.

After that:

Edge ---> US

travels inside Microsoft's private network.

Why is Microsoft's Backbone Faster?

Imagine two roads:

Public Internet
India
  |
ISP1
  |
ISP2
  |
ISP3
  |
ISP4
  |
US

Many unknown routes.

Microsoft Backbone
India Edge
     |
Microsoft Network
     |
US Region

Dedicated high-speed network.

Less congestion.

Less packet loss.

More predictable.

Example

Without Front Door:

India -> ISP -> ISP -> ISP -> US

300ms

With Front Door:

India -> Azure Edge (10ms)

Azure Backbone -> US (150ms)

Total:

160ms

This is where improvement comes from.

Then Why Not Use Application Gateway?

Because Application Gateway is regional.

Suppose:

Application Gateway
Location = East US

All users:

India User --> East US
Europe User --> East US
Australia User --> East US

No intelligent global routing.

Application Gateway Scope

Think:

Inside one region

Example:

East US

Application Gateway
       |
       +--> VM1
       +--> VM2
       +--> VM3

Load balancing inside a region.

Front Door Scope

Think:

Worldwide

Example:

India User --> Southeast Asia
Europe User --> West Europe
US User --> East US

Global routing.

Biggest Benefit: Multi-Region Failover

Suppose:

App1 = East US
App2 = West Europe

With Front Door:

Front Door
    |
    +--> East US
    +--> Europe

If East US fails:

Front Door
    |
    +--> Europe

Automatic failover.

Application Gateway cannot do this globally.

